  "notes": "Built to the user's description, not a specific product. A version of wardrobe-wall-bed-alcove with taller top cabinets and a lower alcove: 189 cm high instead of 205, under top cabinets 60 cm high instead of 44. The joint between the tall and the upper doors of the side columns moves down to the same line, so the wall still reads as one piece; the corners are the original's (34 cm inside). A fitted wardrobe wall, wall to wall and floor to ceiling, for the short wall of Zimmer 13.0 on the bathroom side: side columns with a tall and an upper door each, three top cabinets above the bed, push-to-open doors with mouldings, and 3 cm fillers against the walls. Between the columns, a bed alcove lined in walnut: 177 cm wide, 189 cm high and 57 cm deep, with a book-matched back panel, rounded top corners and a sconce in each side. Made for headboard-box-alcove-177 (177 × 22 × 125 cm) against the back panel and bed-boxspring-160x200 pushed against it: the sconces sit 10 cm above the box, at 135–150 cm and 25 cm in front of the back panel, and there are no niches, as the box's top is the ledge. Bed 160 × 200 with its own headboard (170 cm wide) fits too, with 3.5 cm to spare on each side; a bed's side clearance reaches the cheeks. The width, 292 cm, is the plan value of checklist M04, not yet measured. After measuring, rebuild the items with `npx tsx tools/models/wardrobe-wall-bed-alcove.ts <width>` (it writes all three versions): the side columns take up the difference. No clearance zone: zones always cover a whole side, and one in front would cover the bed. The column doors need about 51 cm in front of them; with the bed in the alcove, its side clearance covers that. Other versions: wardrobe-wall-bed-alcove and wardrobe-wall-bed-alcove-r5. Colorways (fronts / lining / box): white & walnut, the default (lacquer.white / wood.walnut-natural / lacquer.white); all white (lacquer.white everywhere); all walnut (wood.walnut-natural everywhere); chocolate & walnut (lacquer.chocolate / wood.walnut-natural / lacquer.chocolate); light blue & oak (lacquer.light-blue / wood.oak-natural / lacquer.light-blue); off-white & smoked oak (lacquer.off-white / wood.oak-smoked / wood.oak-smoked). Every finish slot takes lacquer, paint and wood, so any other combination works too, including one colour for everything."
